1730–1774, Oliver Goldsmith, Introductory to Switzerland
Though poor the peasant’s hut, his feasts though small,
He sees his little lot the lot of all;
Sees no contiguous palace rear its head
To shame the meanness of his humble shed;

1892, Arthur Christopher Benson, “Knapweed” in Le Cahier Jaune: Poems, Eton: privately printed, p. 62,
Down in the mire he droops his head;
Forgotten, not forgiven.

c. 1924, A.A. Milne, "Happiness", in When We Were Very Young
John had great big waterproof boots on;
John had a great big waterproof hat;
John had a great big waterproof mackintosh --
And that (Said John) is that.

c. 1721-1722, Jonathan Swift, The Progress of Marriage
Young ducklings foster'd by a hen;
But, when let out, they run and muddle

1697, John Dryden (translator), Virgil’s Aeneis, Book 1, lines 776-777, in The Works of Virgil, London: Jacob Tonson, p. 224,
Permit our Ships a Shelter on your Shoars,
Refitted from your Woods with Planks and Oars;
